  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1128 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThursday Night through Tuesday:\n\nThursday night into Friday, low to mid level warm advection is \nexpected ahead of an approaching elongated trough (GFS and NAM)\nor closed sfc wave (ECMWF) over the western Corn Belt. Showers \nand storms are expected on the leading edge of this warm \nadvection. \n\nTonight, deterministic runs of the ECMWF and GFS show a bit\nbetter agreement in the track of the trough and storms for the \nday on Friday and into Friday night. NBM PoPs have responded in\nkind, and have increased since last night, favoring 60 to 80% \nPoPs Friday into early Saturday. However one thing to note is \nthe presence of dry air at the surface ahead of the wave, \nlingering from the preceding high pressure and dry surface \nconditions. This dry air shows up well in the RRFS. Dryness may \nlinger at the surface preceding the rain and cause the \natmosphere to take a while to saturate. While this may be a high\nprobability of precipitation event once rains gets started, it \nmay be low QPF, and hardly a drought buster.\n\nA sfc ridge will then build in behind the exiting trough,\npromoting dry conditions for Sunday through Tuesday. \nTemperatures Friday through Monday will remain seasonable with \nhighs in the upper 70s to low 80s. By the end of the extended \nperiod, it looks like another ridge from the plains leans east \ninto Wisconsin, bringing warmer temperatures in the mid 80s on\nTuesday.\n\nCMiller",
